Top 10 UX + Accessibility Enhancements (Prioritized)
1. Clear room identity & orientation cues

Impact: High
Effort: Medium

Problem:
In an immersive WebGL experience, it’s easy for users to lose track of where they are (which room) and what they can do there. The canvas visuals carry most of this, but they’re not inherently self‑describing.

Enhancement:
Add a small, consistent “room identity strip” anchored in the DOM, e.g. at the top-left or top-center of the immersive page:

    A pill or small banner like:
        “You’re in: Lounge”
        “You’re in: Designer houses”
    Beneath it, one short sentence of guidance:
        “Explore hotspots to discover curated looks and collections.”

Delight & UX details:

    Transition:
        When moving between rooms, fade or slide the pill out and in over ~200–300ms.
        Update text; maybe a tiny underline animation or color accent unique per room.
    A11y:
        Update this text inside an aria-live="polite" region.
        Screen readers hear “Now in Lounge” when room changes.
    Implementation:
        Driven off your existing roomKey in immersive-store.js.
        Room metadata (name, one‑line description) can come from STORE_ROOMS or the JSON config emitted by immersive-canvas.liquid.

2. Discoverable, consistent hotspot affordances

Impact: High
Effort: Medium

Problem:
WebGL hotspots can be visually subtle; users may not notice them or understand what’s interactive.

Enhancement:

    Visual pattern for all hotspots, regardless of their 3D position:
        A small overlay label when hovering/focusing (e.g. “View outfits”, “See designer house”, “Open editorial”).
        A faint pulsing ring or glow around hotspot areas, very subtle to avoid “gamey” feel.
    Hover/focus states:
        On desktop:
            On hover, slightly increase scale and brightness of hotspot marker.
        On keyboard focus:
            Add a clear, high-contrast focus ring (in DOM) that corresponds visually with the hotspot.
    Optional assist layer:
        A small hint toggle (e.g. “Show guides”) in a help menu or onboarding, that temporarily highlights all hotspots with labels.

A11y & UX:

    Every hotspot should have:
        A DOM representation (e.g. a button / link near the canvas) or a parallel focusable element if possible.
        An accessible name (e.g. aria-label="View looks in the Lounge").
    For keyboard users:
        They can move through hotspots via Tab or arrow keys, not just via 3D point-and-click.
    Motion:
        Make pulse subtle and slow, and consider turning off for prefers-reduced-motion.

3. Editorial overlay: “glass panel” hierarchy & motion language

Impact: High
Effort: Medium

Problem:
The editorial overlays fetched into #immersive-editorial-overlay are a crucial storytelling moment, but can easily feel like basic modals or contextless text blocks.

Enhancement:

Establish a consistent “glass editorial card” pattern:

    Layout hierarchy:
        Top: small label with room name or theme (e.g. “Occasions”, “At the Lounge”).
        Main: strong headline + short standfirst (2–3 lines).
        Body: concise text; no more than 2 short paragraphs.
        Bottom: one primary CTA (e.g. “Shop this collection”) and maybe a secondary “Keep exploring”.
    Motion:
        When opened: slide up from bottom or fade-in with a slight blur reveal.
        When closed: fade-out and gently scale down, not snap away.
    Background treatment:
        Slight darkening/blur of the canvas underneath (CSS overlay, not heavy filters if performance-sensitive).

A11y:

    Treat overlay as a dialog:
        Focus moves to first focusable element in overlay.
        aria-modal="true", a meaningful aria-label or aria-labelledby.
        Escape and close button both work.
    Live region:
        Optional aria-live to announce “Editorial panel opened: [headline]”.

Delight:

    Room-specific micro‑touch:
        Tiny visual accent, like an icon or color crumb that matches room character (e.g. more warm tones in Lounge vs. crisp in Storefront).
    Works entirely with DOM/CSS; no need to touch Three.js internals.

4. Smooth, subtle transitions between rooms (View Transitions‑inspired)

Impact: High
Effort: Medium–High

Problem:
Room transitions in WebGL can feel abrupt or disorienting if they’re just cuts or harsh camera moves.

Enhancement:

Align room transitions with a consistent motion language:

    Camera movement:
        Use easing that feels calm (e.g. easeInOutQuad) over ~500–800ms, not longer.
        Keep rotation minimal; prioritize translation and zoom.
    UI overlays:
        Fade out the previous room’s UI hints/labels just before or as transition starts.
        Fade in new room’s identity pill and hints after completion.
    Optional DOM-level effect:
        While the camera animates, subtly fade the entire canvas in/out by a few percent brightness (using CSS opacity or filter), simulating a “soft cut” rather than a jarring shift.

Delight & Comfort:

    For prefers-reduced-motion and/or if you represent a “2D preference” inside 3D:
        Shorten or disable camera motion, replacing it with:
            Immediate jump, but accompanied by a small text cue: “Jumped to Designer houses.”
    Don’t go for “swoopy” or “gamer” transitions; keep it editorial and calm.

5. First‑time onboarding that respects autonomy

Impact: High
Effort: Medium

Problem:
A complex immersive environment is intimidating if users don’t understand the “rules” (how to move, how to interact, what panels are).

Enhancement:

An onboarding overlay in #immersive-onboarding that appears the first time a user enters /pages/immersive and can be dismissed / remembered:

    2–3 concise cards, max:
        “Explore rooms” – short line + little visual cue of clicking a hotspot / moving between rooms.
        “Open stories & products” – show that hotspots reveal editorial panels and collections/products.
        “You’re in control” – mention that they can switch to 2D if needed.
    Each card:
        Clear icon or minimal illustration.
        Simple button: “Next”, “Got it”.
    Footer or small line: “Don’t show this again” (remembered in localStorage).

A11y & UX:

    Use <dialog> or a dialog pattern with focus trapping and esc to close.
    Make sure keyboard and screen readers can step through cards logically.
    Tone: calm, neutral, not “gamified.”

6. Wishlist: emotionally satisfying, not just functional

Impact: Medium–High
Effort: Medium

Problem:
Wishlists often feel like mechanical checklists. In an immersive experience, they’re an opportunity for subtle emotional payoff.

Enhancement (for #immersive-wishlist-panel and wishlist triggers):

    On add to wishlist:
        A very quick micro‑animation on the triggering control:
            Tiny scale up/down or color bloom.
        Small inline confirmation near the control (e.g. “Saved to wishlist”).
    Panel content:
        Empty state:
            “You haven’t saved anything yet. Start in the Storefront or Lounge to discover your favorites.”
            Small illustration or simple icon with gentle motion (SVG wiggle on hover).
        Non-empty:
            Group items by room discovered, or at least display “Found in: Lounge”.
    Actionable from the panel:
        Clear CTA per item (e.g. “View details”, “See in room”).
        A subtle “Keep exploring” button that closes the panel and returns to canvas.

A11y:

    Each add/remove action:
        Announced via an aria-live="polite" region: “Added [item] to wishlist.”
    Ensure that a keyboard-only user can:
        Open the wishlist panel via a visible button.
        Navigate within it logically.

7. Bridge CTAs: trust, clarity, and “tasteful fanfare”

Impact: Medium–High
Effort: Low–Medium

Problem:
Bridge buttons that move between / and /pages/immersive can feel mysterious or risky (“What is this immersive thing? Will it be slow?”).

Enhancement (for snippets/immersive-bridge-btn.liquid and bridge-behavior.js):

    Microcopy:
        Instead of “Enter immersive store” generically, use:
            “Explore in 3D”
            “Step into the immersive store”
        Subtext (small):
            “See curated rooms and collections in an interactive space.”
    First entry “welcome toast”:
        Once a user first lands on /pages/immersive via a bridge CTA, show a small toast:
            “Welcome to the immersive store — you can switch back to classic view anytime.”
        Appears for 3–4 seconds, then fades.
        For motion-sensitive context: minimal motion (fade-only).

Device/connection-aware copy:

    When you recommend staying in 2D:
        “Your connection looks a bit slow. The immersive store may feel heavy. Want to try anyway?”
        Two clear choices:
            Primary: “Stay in classic view”
            Secondary: “Try immersive anyway”
    Avoid fear-based wording; emphasize user control and reassurance.

8. Preference banner: “mode” as personalization, not nagging

Impact: Medium
Effort: Low–Medium

Problem:
A banner that appears on non-immersive pages when immersive_preferred_mode = '3d' can feel like a generic prompt unless it’s framed as your preference.

Enhancement (for the banner in layout/theme.liquid):

    Tone and structure:
        Main text:
            “You prefer the immersive store.”
        Subtext:
            “Switch to the immersive experience to see these products in curated 3D rooms.”
        Buttons:
            Primary: “Go to immersive store”
            Secondary (text link): “Stay in classic view”

    Small, almost playful detail:
        A simple icon or dot that subtly changes color when 3D is the active preference vs 2D.

A11y & UX:

    Doesn’t cover core content; sits at bottom or a slim top bar.
    Dismiss should be clear and persistent (don’t keep showing once user says “Stay in classic view” for that session).

9. Microcopy pass across room names, overlays, and state messages

Impact: Medium
Effort: Low–Medium

Problem:
Disjoint or overly literal copy breaks immersion. You have room names (storefront, lounge, etc.) and editorial text; a unified tone would make the experience feel deliberate.

Enhancement:

    Define a tone: “curated, calm, slightly playful, never shouty.”

    Example microcopy improvements:
        Room switching:
            Instead of silence or nothing, show a small temporary label:
                “Arriving in the Lounge”
        Editorial overlay CTA:
            “Shop this look” instead of “View products.”
        Wishlist:
            “Saved to your wishlist” instead of “Item added.”
        Bridge CTA:
            “Step into the immersive store” instead of “Enter immersive view.”

    Keep sentences short, avoid jargon (“immersive mode” can be “immersive store” or “3D view” in user-facing copy).

This is mostly a text-only change; the architecture already supports it.
10. Subtle a11y‑forward polish: focus, reduced motion, and hints

Impact: Medium
Effort: Medium

Problem:
Even if visually gorgeous, immersive experiences can be frustrating if focus behavior or motion preferences aren’t respected.

Enhancement:

    Focus outlines:
        Ensure every interactive element relevant to the immersive UX (hotspot triggers, overlay buttons, wishlist toggles, bridge CTAs) has:
            A distinct, visible focus style that matches the aesthetic but doesn’t rely on color alone.
    Reduced motion:
        Read prefers-reduced-motion in CSS/JS.
        If reduce:
            Shorten parallax or disable depth-based camera movements.
            Use fades instead of large pans for transitions.
        Present this gently in onboarding or a settings hint if you like (“We keep motion subtle if your device prefers it.”).
    Hinting:
        For keyboard-only users, consider a short inline hint:
            “Press Tab to explore room hotspots.”

These are small but add up to a much more considerate experience.
